From ca10e464cfbaddd719153773dfcc414e26b6bde3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 18:54:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Finish the currency pass, and stop hiding results on 21 more
 calculators
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two loose ends from the last patch, both found by reading the deployed
pages rather than the diff.

The lakh and crore figures were not all spelled out. Several files used
the abbreviations — "$50L", "$1Cr", "$14.2L" — which my search for the
words "lakh" and "crore" walked straight past, so a dollar sign ended up
in front of an Indian magnitude. The amortization page was the worst of
it: its preset buttons read $10L, $25L, $50L, $75L, $1Cr on a page whose
default had just become dollars.

Those presets are now $150K to $750K, at 5.5%-7.5%, with extra payments
of $100-$1,000 a month — the shape of an American mortgage — and the
label is formatted by Intl in compact notation, so the same button reads
"$350K" for a reader in Ohio and "₹3.5L" for one in Pune without either
being hard-coded. Loan Comparison, Loan Prepayment, Sales Commission and
College Cost had their worked examples rebuilt at real US amounts, and
38 numbers still carrying Indian digit grouping were reformatted.

The 9 multi-mode calculators the last patch could not touch are done.
Each has several independent modes, and recomputing one would have
overwritten whichever the visitor had picked; seven already had a
dispatcher that reads the mode, and Grade and Final Grade needed one
written. With 12 more converted along the way, 103 of 117 calculators
now answer as you type.

The 14 still behind a button are date and time tools whose validation
could not be made silent mechanically, plus the invoice generator, where
a button is the right control anyway.

